WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.980
We all know Hollywood loves to recycle ideas.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:08.040
In fact, while we often don't realize it,
studios sometimes even reuse footage — from

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:12.380
old installments in the same franchise or
from completely unrelated movies.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.720
Let's take a look back at some of the most
surprising examples of filmmakers recycling

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.080
parts from another movie.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.060
Life and Spider-Man 3

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.800
The idea of using stock footage in multiple
movies is nothing new, but we received an

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.510
unexpected reminder of that when someone discovered
that a scene in a TV spot for the 2017 sci-fi

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:35.120
film Life is actually taken straight out of
Sam Raimi's Spider-Man 3.

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.760
The scene in question appears about 34 minutes
into Spider-Man 3, with people looking up

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.180
at Gwen Stacy hanging off the edge of a building,
shortly before Spider-Man comes and rescues

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:44.680
her.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.559
A slightly altered version of the scene appeared
around the 26-second mark in a TV spot for

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.900
Life, though it didn't make it into the final
cut.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.560
It's just B-roll footage used for the commercial.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.500
It's no coincidence that the footage came
from Columbia Pictures, since they're the

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.080
studio that produced and distributed both
Spider-Man 3 and Life.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.100
Considering that Venom was one of the villains
in Spider-Man 3, fans of the superhero franchise

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.300
have begun to speculate a possible connection
between the two films.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.600
What if the organism in Life is actually the
Venom symbiote?

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.620
That's a twist worthy of M. Night Shyamalan.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.660
Transformers: Dark of the Moon and The Island

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.820
Recycling doesn't bother Michael Bay.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.180
He's guilty of reusing several scenes from
some of his older films, and often does it

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.460
so seamlessly that it's difficult to tell.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.560
One of the more recent examples can be spotted
in Transformers: Dark of the Moon.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.130
The third Transformers movie features a car
chase scene on a freeway, which includes a

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.460
portion of another car chase sequence Bay
had previously filmed for his 2005 release

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.120
The Island.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.280
Instead of having bounty hunters chasing the
good guys, though, Bay replaced them with

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.080
Decepticons.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.880
The inclusion of The Island's sequence may
have been due to the studio wanting to limit

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:58.480
liability.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.940
The Dark of the Moon set was particularly
accident-prone.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:06.860
The first major accident, due to a snapped
steel cable, left an extra permanently brain

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.420
damaged and partially paralyzed.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:13.450
A second accident occurred a month later,
when the Camaro that portrays Bumblebee collided

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.940
with a police SUV, injuring both drivers.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.400
At that point, someone may have gotten the
bright idea to just use a car chase from another

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:20.900
movie.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.000
Transformers and Pearl Harbor

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.379
Throwing $150 million into a summer blockbuster
movie based on a toy line would have been

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.300
enough to put any studio on edge.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:34.900
So it's understandable that when Michael Bay
was filming the original Transformers, he

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.200
cut corners by reusing a piece of footage
of an aircraft carrier from Pearl Harbor.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.880
While making Harbor in 2000, Bay used the
USS Lexington to represent the USS Hornet

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.640
as well as a Japanese carrier.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.140
Instead of shooting another scene using one
of the Navy's carriers for Transformers, Bay

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.940
just recycled his old footage.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.600
Definitely cheaper than arranging a whole
new shot.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.180
Austin Powers: The Spy Who Shagged Me and
Apollo 13

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.739
When one movie uses footage from another,
they're usually similar in some way.

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:08.120
But there's nothing even remotely alike between
Austin Powers: The Spy Who Shagged Me and

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.000
Apollo 13.

00:03:09.609 --> 00:03:13.340
These two films ordinarily wouldn't find their
titles uttered in the same sentence — were

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.280
it not for one particular scene.

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:20.519
In Austin Powers, Mini-Me escapes with Dr.
Evil to his Moon Base at the end of the movie.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:25.319
To capture him, Austin and Felicity hitch
a ride on Apollo 11 along with one of NASA's

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.580
astronauts, and if the whole sequence seems
out of place, that's because it is.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.920
An eagle-eyed viewer noticed that it was taken
right out of Apollo 13.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.040
It was a sequence created by Academy Award-winning
visual effects designer Robert Legato, who

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.040
received an Oscar nomination for his work.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.020
Rogue One and A New Hope

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.980
In some cases, filmmakers are upfront about
reusing old footage, as was the case with

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.480
Lucasfilm's first Star Wars anthology film.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:55.340
In the original Star Wars, the Rebel Alliance
destroyed the Death Star after obtaining the

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.900
space station's blueprints and discovering
its fatal design flaw: an open exhaust port.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.400
The reason for the vulnerable opening, as
well as the way the Rebels obtained the Death

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.580
Star plans in the first place, became the
basis for Rogue One.

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:12.419
Rogue One's story unfolds days before the
events of A New Hope, and ends mere moments

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.140
before the original film begins.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.040
To give the impression that the film is a
prequel to Episode IV, director Gareth Edwards

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.071
worked with ILM to digitally recreate Peter
Cushing and Carrie Fisher's likenesses, but

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.040
they didn't stop there.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.780
The filmmakers also brought back the original
Gold and Red Leaders, played by Angus MacInnes

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.340
and Drewe Henley, respectively.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.080
Edwards was offered access to the Lucasfilm
vault, so he found old and archived footage

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.500
from A New Hope for the duo.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.120
MacInnes even recorded new dialogue specifically
for Rogue One.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.000
Still, it was a gamble.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:47.230
Edwards was skeptical about using the old
footage at first, but when the two Rebel leaders

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.260
appeared on screen during Rogue One's premiere,
the audience cheered.

